AB  - Urban health and sustainability management is essential for residents, which requires data analytics and visualization tools for decision support. Digital twins are real-time virtual replicas that can map the states and behaviors of physical entities and environments in urban systems. This study proposes a digital twin framework for urban health and sustainability and builds a large-scale air quality management prototype system in Manhattan. The framework comprises: (i) a data-collection module that acquires, cleans, and time-aligns multidimensional streams from CO2/pollutant monitors and other IoT sources, with device metadata, geospatial referencing, and quality-control routines; (ii) an interactive frontend with a 3D digital-twin dashboard, multiscale emissions analytics, and dynamic heatmaps for hotspot detection; and (iii) a backend that couples a Unity WebGL model with a Bayesian spatiotemporal estimator in a state-space formulation. The prototype supports CO2-focused environmental monitoring, spatial analysis, and exploratory scenario evaluation by integrating heterogeneous data, uncertainty-aware estimation, and interactive visualization.
