AB  - Accurate air pollution forecasting is critical for public health and environmental management. Two challenges persist: (1) selecting appropriate lagged input sequences capturing both short-term and long-term pollutant dynamics, and (2) inefficient isolated modeling for each pollutant, limiting scalability. We introduce LagEnsembleForecasting, a deep ensemble framework addressing these challenges. Our approach combines four LSTM-based models trained on distinct lagged sequences (1 h, 6 h, 12 h, 24 h), producing point forecasts and uncertainty estimates (mean ± standard deviation) evaluated across 10 independent random seeds to ensure statistical robustness. This captures short-term fluctuations and broader accumulation trends. Initially developed for PM2.5 forecasting, where the framework achieves a mean MAE of 11.66 ± 0.15μg/m3 and R2 of 0.9548 ± 0.0004 with the tightest confidence interval across all evaluated architectures, models are fine-tuned for additional pollutants (PM10, SO2, CO, O3, NO2), leveraging transfer learning to avoid retraining. Fine-tuning achieves MAE improvements of 8.51%–11.21% over from-scratch baselines across all target pollutants (p<0.001, Bonferroni-corrected), while requiring only half the training epochs and a ten-fold lower learning rate. Experiments on Beijing Multi-Site Air Quality data show LagEnsembleForecasting outperforms single lag-specific models and from-scratch baselines. Results confirm the effectiveness of combining multi-lag ensemble modeling with cross-pollutant transfer learning for real-time forecasting, directly aligned with the increasingly stringent requirements of regulatory frameworks such as the WHO 2021 Air Quality Guidelines and EU Directive 2024/2881/EU.
