AB  - Evaluating the performance of environmental management alternatives requires balancing stakeholder objectives under a wide array of plausible States of the World (SOWs). This paper contributes the MAPS (Mapping Alternative Performance across SOWs) framework, a visual decision-support approach that uses Self-Organizing Maps (SOMs), which simultaneously perform clustering and dimension reduction, to help stakeholders compare management alternatives under deep uncertainty. MAPS clusters large ensembles of SOWs into interpretable groups arranged on a two-dimensional map, onto which alternatives’ performance is superimposed. This enables stakeholders to examine how performance varies across conditions, where their goals are met or missed, and how objective tradeoffs are shifted across the plausible space of system conditions. The framework is demonstrated with a Colorado River Basin case study, illustrating how MAPS can identify potential conflict zones between Upper and Lower Basin stakeholders and lead to identification of compromise alternatives.
