AB  - We propose a novel approach for computing information loss in a watershed based on flow accumulation that physically explains how hydrologically driven information changes with spatial scale. Modifying Shannon’s joint entropy to incorporate flow accumulation over progressively downstream regions of a watershed, the Watershed Joint Entropy index reveals patterns of homogeneity and heterogeneity at different scales across the watershed. A normalized joint entropy expression computed along the longest flow path reflects changes in information loss or gain with cumulative contributing area. Demonstrated on a small watershed with diverse land use, terrain and soil type the approach quantifies information change and landscape diversity along the river network. The same behaviour is confirmed in five additional watersheds with urban land cover ranging from 8% to 58%. The fuzzy Jaccard similarity index in conjunction with flowrates and volumes computed using lumped and distributed applications of standard urban runoff models demonstrate that the proposed measures are able to determine watershed specific scaling thresholds given the spatial distribution of hydrological processes in the catchment.
