AB  - Hydrodynamic flood models play a central role in understanding the spatiotemporal evolution of floods for effective disaster management. Current implementations are limited, as physics-based numerical models are constrained by slow inference times, and data-driven models lack interpretability and physical consistency. Graph Neural Networks (GNNs) provide a physically meaningful framework to represent unstructured spatial domains as graphs, mirroring the node-link structure of numerical models. Unlike other deep learning methods, GNNs require no complex spatial data transformation and can seamlessly integrate physics-based priors. This review synthesizes forty nine peer-reviewed studies on GNN applications in flood modeling, identifying key gaps in physics incorporation, scalability, and cross-domain generalization. Drawing on advances from related fields where GNNs have excelled, we outline strategies to overcome these limitations. GNNs hold significant potential as next-generation flood models by combining physical realism, computational efficiency, and adaptability for real-time flood prediction and risk management.
