AB  - Low-frequency inventories limit near-real-time urban CO2 management. This study develops an interpretable, reusable framework that integrates multisource social-sensing signals into SARIMAX models for daily city-scale CO2 emission forecasting. Daily Carbon Monitor emissions are combined with Baidu search intensity, Weibo-derived sentiment indicators, and topic features, and evaluated for 1- to 49-day-ahead forecasts under an operational no-look-ahead design. Tianjin is used for model development, while Shanghai and Chongqing provide external municipal validation over a common 821-day window from 1 January 2023 to 31 March 2025. The full social-sensing specification reduces mean RMSE relative to the history-only baseline by 10.36% in Tianjin, 9.77% in Shanghai, and 9.64% in Chongqing. GRU and TCN benchmarks show that social-sensing inputs do not automatically improve nonlinear sequence models. Social sensing is therefore interpreted as an event-attention and public-response layer for high-frequency urban carbon decision support.
