AB  - Reliable streamflow modeling is critical as climate change increases variability. This study introduces a data-driven framework that combines Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) and Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) layers to estimate next-day discharge across a dense network of Michigan gauges. The method directly ingests multi-day gridded weather layers, eliminating the need for watershed delineation or other hydrologic preprocessing, and functioning as a digital analog to a distributed process model. When trained on a decade-long record, the network achieved predominantly “good” to “very good” Nash–Sutcliffe and Kling–Gupta efficiencies at most stations, out-performing alternative spatial or temporal clustering approaches in reproducing established atmospheric and ecoregional patterns. Gradient-based saliency, supported by a targeted spatial-perturbation experiment, shows that the model focuses on hydrologically plausible source regions, providing physical interpretability to its estimates. These findings show that pixel-level CNN-LSTM architectures provide skillful, transparent, and efficient streamflow guidance suitable for basin-scale water resource decision support.
