AB  - Developing methods based on widely used probability-based indices to quantify how changing climate patterns alter drought occurrence is crucial. In this study, we developed SPEIChanges, an open-source framework designed to quantify how shifts in the central tendency and dispersion of the precipitation-atmospheric evaporative demand (P-AED) distribution affect drought likelihood of occurrence. Simulation results indicated that the Bayesian Information Criterion should be used within the algorithm. We assessed SPEIChanges performance using observed data and climate projections representing contrasting global conditions. The results showed that SPEIChanges characterizes shifts in expected P-AED values and changes in drought frequency. The method explicitly identifies whether these shifts are driven by changes in central tendency, dispersion, or both features. Notably, it detects and quantifies in probabilistic terms changes in drought frequency even when the widely used Mann-Kendall test fails to detect significant trends.
